    "description": "Iranian cooking encompasses regional traditions as well as widely shared dishes built around grains, vegetables, herbs, dairy products, and meat. Its history includes changing ingredients and techniques, while bread making and shared meals connect food with cultural transmission.",
    "tags": ["Iranian cuisine", "Cooking techniques", "Food heritage", "Bread", "Regional food"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Food & Cooking: Ingredients, Techniques and Traditions | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian cooking through rice, bread, herbs, dairy, shared meals, and the regional traditions behind familiar dishes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "a-diverse-kitchen",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A cuisine with several centers",
        "content": "Iranian food is best approached as a collection of related kitchens. Climate, agricultural resources, and community traditions shape what people prepare, even when dish names travel widely. Rice and wheat bread are important staples, but a meal can also revolve around vegetables, legumes, eggs, or dairy. A useful introduction therefore starts with ingredients and techniques rather than a single supposedly representative national menu. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between a shared repertoire and a uniform diet matters. Recognizing a dish across the country does not establish how often a particular household eats it, how it is seasoned, or which ingredients it can afford. The historical culinary literature used here describes practices; it is not a survey of present-day consumption. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "techniques-and-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cooking methods carry history",
        "content": "Steaming, simmering, and grilling create different kinds of meals. Chelow is plain steamed rice; polow combines rice with other ingredients. These are distinct preparations rather than interchangeable names for any rice dish. [s1]\n\nHistorian Bert Fragner describes the growing importance of elaborate rice cookery in the Safavid period, when Iran was ruled by the Safavid dynasty. His account also discusses older urban foods such as thick soups and the influence of Central Asian contacts on dairy flavorings and pasta preparations. These are historical interpretations based on surviving evidence, rather than proof that every recipe has a continuous, traceable ancient lineage. [s2]\n\nThe same history cautions against treating tradition as immobility. Fragner identifies tomatoes and potatoes as later arrivals in Iranian cooking. A familiar ingredient can become deeply embedded in a cuisine without having been present throughout its history. Questions about a dish's age therefore require evidence about its ingredients, preparation, and name separately. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "herbs-and-dairy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Flavor beyond the main dish",
        "content": "Herbs do more than garnish a finished plate. Dill, called shevid among other local forms, appears in rice with fava beans, herb rice, pickles, and other preparations. The same plant can connect several dish families while performing a different role in each. [s3]\n\nFresh herbs also belong on the table. Sweet basil, or reyhan, is eaten raw as part of sabzi khordan, the herbs served alongside a meal. Iranica's account describes ground-meat kebab served on sangak bread with onion, roasted tomatoes, and seasonal basil. This combination shows why the accompaniments deserve attention alongside the grilled meat. [s4]\n\nDairy preservation has its own culinary consequences. Kashk, a concentrated preserved dairy product described in the cited entry as dried condensed whey, forms the basis of kallajush. Fried onions, herbs, and diluted kashk make a dish eaten with bread. Its historical association with affordable household cooking broadens the picture beyond elaborate rice dishes and hospitality menus. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bread-and-transmission",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Food as shared knowledge",
        "content": "Bread making illustrates how culinary knowledge passes between people. UNESCO's documentation of flatbread making and sharing across Iran and several neighboring countries describes cooperation within families, participation by neighbors, and transmission through apprenticeship. The heritage element concerns social practice as well as a finished bread. [s6]\n\nThe documentation includes household preparation and traditional bakeries, and describes bread shared at ordinary meals and major life events. It should not be read as a claim that every Iranian household follows an identical routine. Nor does a multinational heritage inscription establish exclusive ownership by one country. Its value here is the attention it gives to cooperation, learning, and hospitality. [s6]\n\nThe pages in this section separate dishes, regional cuisines, breads, sweets, tea, seasonal foods, and ceremonial foods so that each can be explored in depth. Read them together: technique explains how a dish works, regional evidence explains a particular version, and social context explains why preparing and sharing it matters.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      }
    ],
