    "description": "Traditional Iranian dishes include rice accompaniments, thick soups, meat-and-legume preparations, stuffed vegetables, and seasoned meatballs. Understanding these families reveals differences in technique that a list of famous dish names can obscure.",
    "tags": ["Traditional dishes", "Khoresh", "Ash", "Abgusht", "Dolma", "Kufteh"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Traditional Iranian Dishes: Stews, Soups, Dolma and Kufteh | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how khoresh, ash, abgusht, dolma, and kufteh differ, and why traditional Iranian dishes allow regional and household variation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "dish-families",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learn the family before the recipe",
        "content": "A useful way to understand an unfamiliar Iranian menu is to recognize categories. Khoresh identifies a family of preparations generally eaten with rice; ash indicates a thick soup; dolma involves stuffing; and kufteh belongs to the meatball family. These names describe culinary structures, leaving room for differences in ingredients and seasoning. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "khoresh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Khoresh: a concentrated partner for rice",
        "content": "Khoresh, also written khoresht, is commonly translated as stew. In the preparation described by Etrat Elahi, meat is browned with onion, while vegetables or herbs may be sautéed separately before the components simmer together. Legumes and dried fruit can also enter the pot. The result is typically less liquid than the English word soup would suggest. [s1]\n\nThe category is flexible. Lamb is traditional in many versions, but poultry and other meats appear; the Persian Gulf regions also have fish preparations, and some local versions use eggs. Khoresh is generally served with plain rice, either drained and steamed chelow or absorption-cooked kateh. This makes the relationship between rice and its accompaniment central to understanding the dish. [s1]\n\nFamiliar names include ghormeh sabzi, gheimeh, fesenjan, and eggplant khoresh. They belong to a repertoire rather than a single fixed formula. Surviving Safavid cooking manuals also record related preparations under the name qalya, providing historical evidence without establishing that today's recipes are unchanged copies. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ash-and-abgusht",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two different kinds of sustaining bowl",
        "content": "Ash can combine grains, legumes, vegetables, noodles, meat, or fruit. Names often identify a defining ingredient: reshteh means noodles, while anar means pomegranate. Tart flavorings and, in some preparations, sweet-and-sour combinations help distinguish varieties. Ash is therefore a broad category, not a synonym for one herb-and-noodle recipe. [s2]\n\nAbgusht is a different meat-based preparation. Nesta Ramazani describes a standard version containing lamb shank, chickpeas, white beans, potato, turmeric, and dried lime. Her historical discussion warns against assuming that an old occurrence of the word proves the existence of the identical modern dish: earlier uses could mean meat broth. Continuity of vocabulary and continuity of recipe require separate investigation. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "stuffing-and-binding",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dolma and kufteh: structure matters",
        "content": "Dolma, a term of Turkish origin, encompasses stuffed vegetables and fruits across a wider Middle Eastern and Mediterranean repertoire. Iranian examples include grape leaves and cabbage leaves, with fillings that can contain meat, rice, herbs, and split peas. Recipes differ between regions and families. Historical evidence also shows changing fillings: the cited entry distinguishes Safavid examples from later rice-containing preparations. [s3]\n\nKufteh usually combines ground meat with herbs, seasonings, and sometimes rice. The mixture must hold together during gentle cooking; this makes proportions and handling part of the cook's skill. Larger examples can enclose dried fruit, nuts, fried onion, or egg. Names may identify an ingredient, size, or association with a place. [s4]\n\nThese distinctions provide a practical reading guide: ask whether a dish is simmered as an accompaniment, assembled as a substantial soup, enclosed in a leaf, or shaped into a bound mixture. That question reveals more than treating every traditional dish as a variation on grilled meat.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
