    "description": "Regional cuisines give shared Iranian dish families distinctive ingredients, flavors, and local associations. Evidence from Gilan, Tabriz, Kermanshah, Fars, and Lorestan illustrates this diversity, although a balanced national survey requires further regional research.",
    "tags": ["Regional cuisine", "Gilan", "Rasht", "Kermanshah", "Tabriz", "Food heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:50Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Regional Cuisines of Iran: Gilan, Kermanshah and Local Variations | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented regional food traditions, from Gilan's sour flavors to Kermanshah's culinary repertoire and locally named soups and meatballs."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-regional-differences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Look for variation within familiar dishes",
        "content": "Regional comparison becomes useful when it moves beyond assigning one famous food to each province. A dish family may travel, while its ingredients, acidity, texture, or serving context change. Locally named soups and meatballs offer examples of this relationship between shared techniques and particular places. They should not be treated as evidence that a recipe belongs exclusively within a modern administrative boundary. [s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gilan",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gilan: rice, green ingredients, and sour flavors",
        "content": "Gilan lies along Iran's Caspian coast. Christian Bromberger's account emphasizes rice, garden vegetables, herbs, eggs, poultry, and fish, together with a pronounced preference for sour flavors. His examples include mirza ghasemi, combining eggplant, garlic, tomato, and egg, and baghala ghatogh, a bean preparation. These combinations demonstrate why a regional cuisine cannot be reduced to its best-known meat dish. [s1]\n\nVariation also occurs within a familiar name. Bromberger describes Rasht-style fesenjan, a walnut-and-pomegranate preparation, as particularly sour, contrasting it with sweeter versions elsewhere. His account is a dated ethnographic description, not a rule governing every contemporary cook. [s1]\n\nUNESCO's profile of Rasht, Gilan's principal city, highlights local fish, seasonal produce, and cooking in gamaj clay pots. It also describes food-related training and heritage activities. These institutional statements establish how the city presents and promotes its culinary heritage; they do not independently measure the economic success of those programs. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "northwest-and-west",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Northwestern and western examples",
        "content": "Kufteh Tabrizi takes its name from Tabriz in northwestern Iran. Iranica places it among the larger forms of kufteh, alongside varieties named for ingredients or size. The example shows how a place name can distinguish a preparation within a much wider family of seasoned meatballs. It does not establish that every meatball made in Tabriz follows one recipe. [s3]\n\nA Kermanshah city presentation hosted by the Macao Government Tourism Office identifies saji bread, beji pastries, rice-flour cookies described in English as rice bread, lamb kebab skewers, Abbasali soup, and khalal stew as local specialties. The presentation connects the city's food with its mountainous setting and cultural diversity. As promotional source material, it is useful for identifying the city's stated repertoire, but its expansive claims about the number and antiquity of dishes require independent checking. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "soups-and-further-coverage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional map remains incomplete",
        "content": "Soups provide another route into local differences. Iranica identifies ash-e karda with Fars and ash-e tarkhina with Lorestan, while describing ash as a category found throughout Iran. Such examples connect a shared format with particular ingredients and regional names. [s5]\n\nThe evidence assembled here is strongest for Gilan and selected western and northwestern examples. It is insufficient for a balanced account of the Persian Gulf coast, Khuzestan, Baluchistan, Khorasan, central Iran, Mazandaran, and mobile pastoral communities. Those traditions need direct regional scholarship and documented culinary accounts before this page can represent the country adequately. The present examples are an introduction, not a complete culinary map.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
